{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "41",
    "case_summary": "A 24-year-old Tunisian woman was admitted to the hospital 2 weeks after vaginal delivery with a 10-day history of pain in the right leg, right iliac fossa, and flank. These symptoms worsened over the following days. The patient developed chest pain, tachycardia, dyspnea, and fever, which increased in severity. She did not respond to antibiotic therapy. Her past medical history included recurrent oral and genital aphthous, pseudofolliculitis, and erythema nodosum in the lower extremities. She had previously been on colchicine therapy but had discontinued it on her own. Physical examination upon admission revealed a fever of 38.5°C, oral ulcers on the lips, hypopigmented genital scars, and pseudofolliculitis. Heart sounds and bilateral lung areas were normal upon auscultation. Laboratory tests indicated an inflammatory condition with a high erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 130 mm in the first hour, a C reactive protein level of 78.3 mg/dl (normal range 0–5 mg/dl), a hemoglobin value of 11 g/dl, and a leukocyte count of 5400/mm3 with a normal differential count. Urinalysis, liver and kidney function tests were normal; antinuclear antibody tests were negative; platelet count, protein C, protein S, and antithrombin III levels were within normal limits; and anticardiolipin antibodies and lupus anticoagulant were absent. Her plasma total homocysteine level was 17 μmol/l (normal < 10 μmol/l). Chest radiography and electrocardiogram were normal except for sinus tachycardia.\n\nColor Doppler echography and abdominal computed tomographic scan (CT) revealed an ascending thrombosis of the iliac and right ovarian veins. Chest helical CT revealed a bilateral mural thrombus of segmental pulmonary arteries and a triangular area of opacity representing a pulmonary infarct at the posterior segment of the right lower lobe.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
